# Crontab for the `deploy` user on the VPS — this file is the source of truth.
# The deploy workflow copies it to /opt/adwear/crontab and installs it with
# `crontab`, so live edits on the server are overwritten by the next deploy.

# Nightly DB + files backup
15 3 * * * /opt/adwear/backup.sh >> /opt/adwear/backups/backup.log 2>&1

# Malfini syncs. flock skips a run while the previous one still holds the
# lock. Daily order: new products (04:00) get prices (05:00) and go live with
# the next hourly stock run — until then VisibilitySyncer keeps them hidden.
#
# Stock skips only the 23:30 UTC slot. /product/availabilities returns one row
# per code stamped with the current date and, with includeFuture=false, only
# rows for today; Malfini rebuilds that snapshot overnight and while it does,
# every row comes back with quantity 0 — indistinguishable from a real
# sell-out. Writing those zeros used to deactivate the whole catalogue until
# the next run restored it.
# Measured by sampling the feed every 30 min for 24h (2026-08-09/10):
#   21:30Z  18.9% zeros, all dated D      good
#   22:00Z  18.9% zeros, dates D + D+1    good (date rolls over gradually,
#                                         so date alone is NOT a usable signal)
#   23:00Z  100% zeros, all dated D+1     UNUSABLE
#   23:30Z  100% zeros, all dated D+1     UNUSABLE
#   00:00Z  18.9% zeros, all dated D+1    good
# So the bad window is ~23:00-23:59 UTC — one hour, not the whole night. The
# old 7h runtimes made it look wider: the 23:30 run spent all night *writing*
# zeros it fetched in one instant. StockSyncer also refuses any feed that is
# >=90% zeros, so this schedule is defence in depth rather than the only guard.
30 0-22 * * * cd /opt/adwear && flock -n /tmp/malfini-stock.lock docker compose exec -T -u www-data app php -d memory_limit=-1 bin/console adwear:malfini:sync-stock >> logs/cron-stock.log 2>&1
0 4 * * * cd /opt/adwear && flock -n /tmp/malfini-products.lock docker compose exec -T -u www-data app php -d memory_limit=-1 bin/console adwear:malfini:sync-products >> logs/cron-products.log 2>&1
0 5 * * * cd /opt/adwear && flock -n /tmp/malfini-prices.lock docker compose exec -T -u www-data app php -d memory_limit=-1 bin/console adwear:malfini:sync-prices >> logs/cron-prices.log 2>&1

# Forced product refresh (--force, applies mapping/data changes to existing
# products) is intentionally NOT scheduled — run it manually when needed.

# Housekeeping — nothing else prunes logs. With APP_ENV=prod they stay small,
# but cap them anyway: drop app logs older than 14 days (inside the container),
# and truncate any malfini cron log on the host that grows past 20 MB.
#
# The DB log table (ps_log) needs its own prune: core ships no retention policy
# for it at all, only the BO "Erase all logs" button, which is a TRUNCATE. And
# it is not just a disk concern — PrestaShopLogger::isPresent() runs an
# unindexed COUNT(*) on every single write, so a large ps_log slows the stock
# sync until flock starts skipping runs. prune-logs.sh keeps 90 days and caps
# the table at 200k rows; see the header there. Runs at 03:45, i.e. AFTER the
# 03:15 backup, so the nightly dump still holds a copy of whatever it removes.
45 3 * * * /opt/adwear/prune-logs.sh >> /opt/adwear/logs/cron-prune-logs.log 2>&1
50 3 * * * cd /opt/adwear && docker compose exec -T -u www-data app find var/logs -name '*.log' -mtime +14 -delete >/dev/null 2>&1
55 3 * * * find /opt/adwear/logs -name '*.log' -size +20M -exec truncate -s 0 {} \; >/dev/null 2>&1
